# 4. SECURITY GOVERNANCE HIERARCHY

The security engine must support the following configuration hierarchy.

## 4.1 Mandatory configuration levels

The following levels are mandatory:

1. Global / Enterprise.
2. Company.
3. Branch.
4. Module.

<GLOBAL / ENTERPRISE>
        |
        v
<COMPANY>
        |
        v
<BRANCH>
        |
        v
<MODULE>

Every company, branch and module must support security policy evaluation.

The system must support multiple companies, multiple branches and multiple modules.

## 4.2 Optional configuration levels

The following are optional extensions:

5. Page / Screen.
6. Individual User.

<OPTIONAL PAGE / SCREEN>
<OPTIONAL INDIVIDUAL USER>

Page/Screen configuration must be independently configurable.

Individual User configuration must be independently configurable.

Neither optional feature may be a prerequisite for normal operation.

If both optional features are disabled, the security engine must operate fully using the effective Module policy.

If only Page/Screen configuration is enabled, User configuration remains optional.

If only User configuration is enabled, Page/Screen configuration remains optional.

If neither is configured, the system must continue to work without errors or missing-policy failures.

Do not force administrators to configure every page or every user.

## 4.3 Effective policy hierarchy

Evaluate the policy in this order:

Global → Company → Branch → Module → Page/Screen (optional) → Individual User (optional).

The server must calculate the effective policy.

The frontend must consume and enforce the effective policy.

The backend must independently enforce authorization for sensitive operations.

## 4.4 Policy inheritance

Each security control must support an appropriate configuration state:

- INHERIT.
- ENABLED.
- DISABLED, where permitted.
- MANDATORY.
- OPTIONAL.
- NOT_APPLICABLE.
- AUTHORIZED_EXCEPTION, where supported.

Do not interpret an unconfigured value as disabled.

Do not use simplistic last-write-wins policy evaluation.

Mandatory restrictions cannot be weakened by lower-level configuration.

For each effective setting, display:

- Effective value.
- Source level.
- Source configuration.
- Mandatory status.
- Override status.
- Exception status.
- Policy version.

## 4.5 Policy exceptions

Authorized exceptions must require:

- Authorized approver.
- Reason.
- Scope.
- Start date.
- Expiry date.
- Audit history.

An expired exception must automatically stop applying.

A user must not be allowed to modify their own security policy.

