# 11. WEBCAM-BASED LOCAL AI DETECTION ENGINE

Implement optional local computer vision to identify visible recording devices around the workstation.

The system must use real model inference.

It must not simulate detection.

## 11.1 Technology evaluation

Evaluate suitable production-supported technologies, including:

- ONNX Runtime Web.
- WebGPU.
- WASM fallback.
- Verified object-detection models.
- Local model storage.
- Browser MediaDevices API.

Choose the actual technology after evaluating:

- Commercial-use licence.
- Model provenance.
- Model accuracy.
- Model size.
- Browser compatibility.
- Hardware compatibility.
- Performance.
- Memory usage.
- CPU/GPU utilization.
- Security.

Do not choose a model merely because it is popular.

Do not download unverified models.

## 11.2 Detection targets

Evaluate detection of:

- Mobile phones.
- Visible cameras.
- Recording devices.
- Suspicious device presence.
- Repeated device visibility.

Where the model supports it, evaluate device orientation and proximity.

Do not claim that the model can determine that a photograph is actually being taken merely because a phone is visible.

## 11.3 Privacy requirements

Default configuration:

- Local webcam processing.
- No webcam frame upload.
- No webcam video recording.
- No continuous video retention.
- No facial recognition.
- No biometric identification.
- No identity inference.
- Only necessary detection metadata is transmitted.

Camera access must require valid user permission.

Display an explicit camera-active indicator.

Stop the camera when monitoring is no longer required.

If camera permission is denied, apply the configured security policy. Do not silently bypass the requirement.

## 11.4 Detection workflow

1. Verify that webcam monitoring is enabled by policy.
2. Obtain browser permission.
3. Initialize the local inference runtime.
4. Load the verified model.
5. Process frames locally.
6. Detect supported object classes.
7. Evaluate confidence.
8. Apply persistence and false-positive suppression.
9. Generate an event.
10. Send minimum required event metadata to the backend.
11. Evaluate risk.
12. Execute the configured response.
13. Record the event.

## 11.5 Risk classification

Support:

- Informational.
- Low.
- Medium.
- High.
- Critical.

Configure:

- Confidence threshold.
- Detection persistence.
- Escalation window.
- False-positive suppression.
- Response action.

Do not automatically terminate a user's session based solely on one unverified low-confidence detection.

## 11.6 Performance

Measure:

- Inference latency.
- CPU usage.
- GPU usage where applicable.
- Memory usage.
- Detection accuracy.
- False-positive rate.
- False-negative rate.

Provide a configurable frame-processing frequency.

Avoid unnecessary continuous full-resolution inference.

